00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:09.550
Morning post.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:10.820
City desk?

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.300
Just a moment. I'll connect you.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:14.760
Anybody ask me, I'm down at the courthouse.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.020
Morning post.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:16.520
Elevator.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.300
Hello, Hildy.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.320
Oh, hiya, Skinny.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.800
Hello, Ruth. Hello, Maisie.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.380
Hello, Hildy. How are you?

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.780
Tell me, is the Lord of the Universe in?

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.340
he's in in a bad humor somebody must have stolen the crown jewels shall we announce oh no no i'll

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:39.500
blow my own walls he's in ruse you better wait here i'll be back in 10 minutes even 10 minutes

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:51.410
is a long time to be away from you what did you say huh go on oh go ahead oh i just said even 10

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:55.670
minutes is a long time to be away from you i heard you the first time i like it that's why i

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.970
asked you to say it again the sand being spoiled the gentleman i'm going into

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.200
See, you did very little spoiling.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.880
I'd like to spoil him just once.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.000
You sure you don't want me to go in with you?

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:04.840
Oh, no, I can handle it.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.280
Well, if things get rough, remember, I'm here.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:07.460
I'll come a-running, partner.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.140
Well, hello, Jim.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:11.100
Well, hello, Hildy.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.800
Hi, Hildy. Welcome back.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:14.620
Hello, Hildy. How have you been?

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:15.040
Good.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:16.280
Hi, Hildy.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.360
Hello, Beatrice. How's advice to the love-lorn?

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.000
Fine. My cat just had kittens again.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:21.560
It's her own fault.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.760
Yeah, hello, Hildy. Glad to see you, Mike.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:23.380
Glad to see you.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:23.740
What?

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:24.320
Hi, Jim.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.760
You still around?

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.960
All right. A little more around her chin, boys.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.680
What do you want? Your ex-wife is here. Do you want to see her?

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.750
Hello, Hildy. Hello, Walter.

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:41.580
Hi, Hildy. Oh, hello, Louie.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.580
How's the big slot machine, King? Oh, I ain't

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.890
doing that no more. I'm retired. You know what I mean?

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.830
Say, Walter. I'm busy, Duffy. Hello, Hildy.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.970
Hello, Duffy. Get going, I'm busy. I thought you ought to

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.710
know that the governor didn't sign that reprieve. What?

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:53.810
And tomorrow morning, Earl Williams dies and makes a sucker

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.780
out of us. Well, what do you got to do?

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.500
Get the governor on the phone. I can't. Why not?

00:02:59.820 --> 00:02:59.960
Can't?

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:02.620
How many places to fish are there?

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.500
Well, at least two, the Atlantic and Pacific.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:05.900
All right, that simplifies it, doesn't it?

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.000
Get him on the phone.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:07.780
And tell him what?

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.140
Quiet, Duffy. He's thinking.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.710
Tell him if you reprieve Earl Williams, we'll support him for Senator.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:14.110
What?

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:16.430
Tell him the Morning Post will be behind him, hook, line and sinker.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.130
But you can't do that.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:17.450
Why not?

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.030
Because we've been a democratic paper for over 20 years.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.870
All right, after we get the reprieve, we'll be democratic again.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:23.410
Oh, won't.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:24.690
Go on, Duffy, get going.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:27.310
Remember, the Morning Post expects every city editor to do his duty.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:27.710
All right, all right.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:28.790
You two, Louie, get out of here.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:29.350
Okay, boy.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.640
Walter, I see you're still at it.

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.770
It's the first time I ever double-crossed the governor. What can I do for you?

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:41.910
Would you mind if I sat down?

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.580
There's been a lamp burning in the window for you, honey. Here.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.440
Oh, I jumped out that window a long time ago, Walter.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.490
Yeah? In the match.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:08.480
How long is it? Miss Walt?

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.440
You know what. How long is it since we've seen each other?

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.360
Oh, well, let's see.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.320
I spent six weeks in Reno, then Bermuda.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:20.500
About four months, I guess. Seems like yesterday to me.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:22.900
Maybe it was yesterday, Hildy.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.520
Been seeing me in your dreams?

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.150
No, no. Mama doesn't dream about you anymore.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:28.470
You wouldn't know the old girl now.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:29.970
Ah, yes, I would. I'd know...

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.100
You're repeating yourself, Walter. That's the speech you made the night you proposed.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.600
I know that you still remember it.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.020
If I didn't remember it, I wouldn't have divorced you.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.860
I wish you hadn't done that, Hildy.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:42.500
Done what?

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:45.660
Divorce me. Makes a father lose all faith in himself.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.700
Almost gives him a feeling he wasn't wanted.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.940
Now, look, Junior, that's what divorces are for.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:52.920
Nonsense. You've got an old-fashioned idea.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.480
Divorce is something that lasts forever till death do us part.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.760
Divorce doesn't mean anything nowadays, Hildy.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.720
Just a few words. Mum would over you by judge.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.720
If we've got something between us, nothing can change.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.970
Mm, well, I suppose you're right in a way, Walter.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:06.670
Sure, I'm right.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.400
I am fond of you, you know.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:09.350
And a girl?

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:11.600
I often wish you weren't such a stinker.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.020
Latin, I suppose. You must come up and meet my mother.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:16.520
She'd like that, I'm afraid.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:18.680
Then why on earth did you promise not to fight the divorce

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.020
and do everything you possibly could to gum up the whole works?

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.320
Well, I meant to let you go, Hildy, but you know how it is.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.500
You never miss the water till the well runs dry.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.660
Oh, a big fat lummox like you hiring an aeroplane to write,

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:29.980
Hildy, don't be hasty.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.900
Remember my dimple, Walter.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.130
Delayed our divorce 20 minutes while the judge went out to watch it.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.710
Well, I don't want to brag, but I've still got the dimple and in the same place.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.620
Look, Hildy, I only acted like any husband who didn't want to see his home broken up.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:43.100
What home?

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.280
What home? Don't you remember the home, I promised you?

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:48.390
Sure I do. That was the one we were to have right after the honeymoon.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:50.170
That honeymoon.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.470
Was it my fault? Did I know that coal mine was gonna have another cave-in?

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:57.130
I intended to be with you on our honeymoon, Hildy. Honest, I did.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.970
All I know is that instead of two weeks in Atlantic City with my...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.300
In my bridegroom, I spent two weeks in a coal mine with John Krupsky.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.640
You don't deny that, do you, Walter?

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.660
Deny it? I'm proud of it. We've beaten the whole country on that story.

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.780
I suppose we did. That isn't what I got married for.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:12.660
Oh, what is the good of... Look, now, look, Walter,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.440
what I came up here to tell you

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.040
is that you must stop phoning me a dozen times a day,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.460
sending me 20 telegrams.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:20.740
I write a beautiful telegram, don't I? Everybody says so.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:22.140
Are you gonna listen to what I have to say?

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.980
Look, look, what's the use of fighting, Hildy? I'll tell you what you do.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.240
You come back to work on the paper,

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.160
if we find we can't get along in a friendly fashion,

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.280
we'll get married again.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.300
Certainly. I haven't any hard feelings.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:36.300
Oh, Walter, you're wonderful in a loathsome sort of way.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.040
Now, will you please be quiet just long enough for me to tell you what I came up here to say?

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:40.960
I have a lunch date already.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:41.660
I'll break it.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:44.760
I cannot break it. Will you take your hands off me? What are you playing, osteopath?

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:45.300
Temper? Temper?

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:50.230
Oh, listen, Walter. You are no longer my husband and no longer my boss.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:51.600
And you're not going to be my boss.

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:52.950
What's that supposed to mean?

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:54.190
Just what I say.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:56.050
You mean you're not coming back to work on the paper?

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:57.790
You are right, Mr Burns, for the first time today.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.970
Ah, got a better eye.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.500
All right, go on, take it, work for somebody else. That's the gratitude I get.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:05.280
Oh, I wish you'd stop panting.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:08.340
What were you when you came here five years ago, a little college girl from a school journalism?

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:09.740
I took a doll-faced hick.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.480
Well, you wouldn't take me if I hadn't been doll-faced.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:14.540
Why should I? It would be a novelty to have a face around here a man could look at without shuddering.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:14.940
Listen, Walter...

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.500
Listen, I made a great reporter out of you, Hildy, but you won't be half as good on any other paper, and you know it.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.800
We're a teen. That's what we are. You need me, and I need you.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:29.980
Listen, Walter, please. The paper's gonna have to get along without me. So are you. Just didn't work out.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:35.100
It would have worked out if you'd been satisfied with just being editor and reporter, but not you.

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:36.600
You had to marry me. Spoil everything.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:39.480
I wasn't satisfied. I suppose I proposed to you.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.720
Well, you practically did, making goo-go-eyes at me for two years until I broke down.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:43.540
Oh, Walter.

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:46.410
And I still claim I was tight the night I proposed to you.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.530
If you'd have been a gentleman, you'd have forgotten all about it, but not you.

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:49.230
Why, you...

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.630
You're losing your eye. You used to be at a bitch better than that.

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:55.480
Hello? Yeah? What? Sweeney? What can I do for you?

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:58.340
Wait a minute. I'm not Sweeney. I'm Duffy.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:07:59.970
Listen, Sweeney, you can't do that to me.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:06.620
not today of all days what's the matter with you are you a loony jumping jehoshaphat now listen

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:15.250
sweetie this is no time oh i suppose so yes if you have to you have to he had to how do you like

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:20.370
that everything happens to me 365 days in a year and this has to be the day what's the matter

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:24.710
walter sweeney dead i might just as well be the only man on the paper that can write and he picks

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:28.500
today to have a baby well he didn't do it on purpose did he i don't care what he did or not

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.900
It's supposed to be coming in the old Williams case.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.980
And where is he? Walking up and down in the hospital.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.720
Is there no sense of honor in this country?

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.040
Well, haven't you got anybody else?

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.790
No. No, there's nobody else on the paper that can write.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.420
This'll break me, unless Hildy.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:46.350
You've got to help me. I'm busy.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.740
This'll bring us back together again, just the way we used to be.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:51.820
That's just what I'm afraid of, any time, any place, anywhere.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:53.960
Don't mock me. This is bigger than anything that ever happened to us.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.200
Don't do it for me. Do it for the paper.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.180
Scrams, Bengali.

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:57.910
If you won't do it for love, how about money?

00:08:58.730 --> 00:08:59.890
Forget the other offer. I'll raise you $20.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.880
Listen to me, you great, big, bumble-headed, bad fool.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.700
I'm making 35 bucks and not a cent more.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:04.500
Where are you gonna listen?

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.730
Well, good grief, how much is that other paper gonna pay you?

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:07.630
There isn't any other paper.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.150
Oh, well, in that case, I raise it off.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:10.370
You go back to your old salary and I'm like,

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:11.010
how do you like that?

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:11.970
Trying to backjack me?

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:12.530
I'm busy.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:14.570
It's here. It's a ring. Take a good look at it.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:15.170
You know what it is?

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:16.270
It's an engagement ring.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:20.760
Engagement ring?

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:27.700
I'd tell you right away, but you would start reminiscing.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:29.880
I'm getting married, Walter.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.070
and I'm also getting as far away from the newspaper business as I can get.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:34.550
What?

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:36.480
I'm through.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.210
You can't quit the newspaper business.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:39.950
Oh, why not?

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.300
I know you, Hildy. I know what quitting would mean to you.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:42.900
What would it mean?

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:43.520
Yeah, but kill you.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:45.900
You can't sell me that, Walter Burns.

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:47.560
Who says I can't? You're a newspaper man.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.270
That's why I'm quitting. I want to go someplace where I can be a woman.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:51.190
You mean be a traitor.

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:52.770
A traitor? A traitor to what?

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:54.730
A traitor to journalism. You're a journalist, Hildy.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.600
A journalist? Now, what does that mean?

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:59.520
Peeking through keyholes? Chasing after fire engines?

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.720
Waking people up in the middle of the night to ask them if Hitler's going to start another war.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:04.040
Stealing pictures off old ladies.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.020
I know all about reporters, Walter.

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:08.580
A lot of daffy buddinskis running around without a nickel in their pockets.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.460
And for what? So a million hired girls, most of them as wise, will know what's going on.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:12.100
Why, I...

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:14.490
Oh, what's the use?

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:17.330
Walter, you wouldn't know what it means to...

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:20.770
Well, I want to be respectable and live a halfway normal life.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.490
Point is, I'm through.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:27.910
Where'd you meet this man?

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:29.400
Bermuda.

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:34.420
Rich, huh? Well, he's not what you call rich. Makes about 5,000 a year.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.030
What's his line? He's in the insurance business.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.750
Insurance business? Well, that's a good honest business, isn't it?

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:43.910
Oh, certainly is honest. It's also adventurous. It's romantic.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.510
Listen, Hildy, I can't picture you being surrounded by policies.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:48.390
Policies with a right of imposition. Doesn't make sense.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:52.170
I can, and I like it, what's more. Besides, he forgets the office when he's with me.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:55.880
He doesn't treat me like an errand boy, either, Walter. He treats me like a woman.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:59.140
It does, does, eh? How did I treat you? Like a water buffalo?

00:10:59.400 --> 00:10:59.780
I don't know.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.930
But I do know about him. He's kind and he's sweet and he's considerate.

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:05.840
He wants a home and children.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.760
Sounds more like a guy I ought to marry. What's his name?

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:11.300
Uh, Baldwin. Bruce Baldwin.

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:13.520
Baldwin, Baldwin, Baldwin. Oh, I knew a Baldwin once.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.320
A horse thief in Mississippi. Couldn't be the same fella.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.920
You're now talking about the man I'm marrying tomorrow.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:21.640
Tomorrow? As soon as that?

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:29.540
I'll appear to tell you.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.900
Guess there isn't any more to the story. So long, Walter.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:37.180
So long, Hildy.

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:39.370
Better luck to you next time.

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:41.960
Thanks. Oh, Hildy.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:42.760
Huh?

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:48.060
Kind of took the wind out of my sails.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.200
Look, honey, I just want to wish you everything I couldn't give you.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:53.740
Thank you, Walter.

